# Film & Theatre Taste Considerations & Philosophical Probes
#
# Living breath vs sculpted light, the ethics of the gaze, performer vulnerability, and wonder probes in dramatic arts.

#>spw_cache_univ_film_theatre_taste
#:cache #!film_and_theatre
#:layer #!pragmatics

^"intent"{
  ~#goal: "Synthesize the aesthetic tensions, somatic philosophies, and ethical responsibilities that unite and distinguish live dramatic theatre and recorded cinematic storytelling."
  ~#resonance: "Living ephemeral presence, the immortalized cinematic gaze, radical emotional vulnerability, and anti-cynical artistic leadership"
}

^["the_living_communion_vs_the_sculpted_shadow"]{
  epistemic_duality: "Theatre and cinema are twin sisters born from the same primeval campfire, but they inhabit radically different dimensions of time."
  distinctions: #[
    "The Ephemeral Breath of the Stage: A theatrical performance exists only in the present moment; once the curtain falls, that exact configuration of light, sound, and living emotion is lost forever to memory",
    "The Immortality of the Film Frame: Cinema captures human life and preserves it in amber; an actor who died sixty years ago can still look through the lens directly into our eyes and make our hearts race",
    "The Boundary of Distance: In theatre, the actor must project their soul across fifty feet of physical space to the back balcony; in cinema, the camera draws near until a pupil dilation fills a fifty-foot screen"
  ]
}

^["the_radical_vulnerability_of_the_actor"]{
  the_sacred_instrument: "The actor is the only artist whose instrument is their own living nervous system, body, and emotional history."
  ethical_truths: #[
    "Acting is Truth, Not Deception: Bad acting is pretending to feel; great acting is the terrifying bravery to tell the absolute emotional truth under imaginary circumstances",
    "The Sanctuary of the Rehearsal: The rehearsal hall must be an inviolable psychological sanctuary where performers can fail, look foolish, weep, and take wild risks without fear of mockery",
    "The Ethics of Intimacy & Consent: Physical and sexual scenes are not improvisational free-for-alls; they require the same rigorous, repeatable choreography and boundaries as a broadsword fight"
  ]
}

^["resisting_the_content_machine"]{
  cultural_crisis: "Late-stage commercial entertainment reduces the ancient spiritual art of drama to algorithmic 'content' designed solely to harvest dopamine and maintain streaming subscriptions."
  artistic_resistance: #[
    "The Defense of the Tragic: Reclaiming tragedy—allowing stories to end in grief, loss, and unresolvable mystery rather than sanitized corporate comfort",
    "The Idiosyncratic Human Voice: Championing playwrights and filmmakers who write about specific, flawed, eccentric human beings rather than focus-grouped archetypes",
    "The Collective Gathering in the Dark: Defending the cinema hall and the theatrical auditorium as the last secular temples where humans gather in the dark to confront what it means to be alive"
  ]
}

^"taste"{
  ~#taste: "To make theatre or film is to say to another human being: 'You are not alone in the dark; I have felt that heartbreak too.'"
  ~#wonder: "Why does sitting in a dark room with three hundred strangers watching flickering shadows of light heal grief that hours of solitary meditation cannot touch?"
}

^"invariants"{
  "Dramatic direction must honor the psychological autonomy and physical safety of actors across all scenes.",
  "Artistic critique must focus on creative craft choices and storytelling efficacy rather than personal artistic identity.",
  "Theatrical and cinematic spaces must remain accessible and hospitable to diverse audiences and working-class patrons.",
}

#>wonder_film_theatre_taste_1
?["How does the collective acoustic breathing of an audience synchronize during an intense theatrical silence?"]{
  #:depth #!somatic ~#lens(collective_respiratory_entrainment)
  ~#hypothesis: "During a shared dramatic pause in a live theatre performance, audience respiratory rates synchronize within three seconds, creating an objective physiological state of collective entrainment that is physically impossible to experience when watching a movie alone on a phone."
  !probe{ "Measure multi-seat respiratory sensor bands and heart-rate variability across 200 audience members during a high-stakes dramatic monologue." }
  $%[audience.respiratory_synchrony_index, physiology.collective_hrv_coherence]
}

#>wonder_film_theatre_taste_2
?["What is the neurological threshold where an audience stops perceiving edited cuts and begins experiencing cinematic time as continuous reality?"]{
  #:depth #!neuroscience ~#lens(cinematic_event_segmentation)
  ~#hypothesis: "When an edit aligns with an actor's natural blink or sudden head turn, the viewer's visual cortex suppresses saccadic perception for 80 milliseconds, seamlessly bridging two completely different camera angles into a single unbroken neural event."
  !probe{ "Conduct high-density EEG event-related potential (ERP) recordings in subjects viewing continuous action cut on movement vs cut during static holds." }
  $%[neuroscience.saccadic_suppression_erp_uv, perception.cut_invisibility_rating]
}
